.SH NAME
mexec - my exec, creates a pipeline of commands

.SH SYNOPSIS
mexec [FILE]

.SH DESCRIPTION
Executes a list of commands where standard output (stdout) from the previous
command is piped to standard input (stdin) of the next command.
Commands are read from stdin by default, or optionally from a
.I FILE
passed as an argument to
.BR mexec .
.PP
Each input line is considered a command line with space-separated arguments.
Command lines shall not be longer than 1024 bytes each.
Empty lines and quotation marks are not supported.

.SH SOLUTION
Command lines are read from an input stream, either from stdin or from an open
file. Each command line is parsed into an array of arguments, where the first
element is the command itself and the rest of the elements are arguments for the
command. The first parsed command line is stored, then next command
line is parsed and a pipe is created with the pipe() system call. This pipe is
the output pipe for the previous command and the input pipe for the next command.
When the output pipe has been created the previous command is executed with the
read end of the input pipe attached to stdin for the command process, using the
dup2() system call, and the write end of the output pipe is attached to stdout
for the command process. For the very first command in the pipeline there is
no input pipe and stdin remains unaltered instead. The next command is read and
a pipe between previous and next command is created while there are commands to
read. The final command is executed with the read end of its input pipe attach
to stdin of the command process and stdout of the command process remaining
unaltered.
Once the whole pipeline has been set up, the main process waits for all child
command processes to terminate before exiting.
.PP
